                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "endosymbioses"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a0fe21c1-6950-3f02-b144-092ca13073cb",
                        "entry_id": "f5c4c3d0-e4bf-3713-b70d-499901e256df",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The condition of living within the body or cells of another organism; an instance of an organism so living."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2e63a956-904d-42db-bd09-e44f0afc828f",
                                "sense_id": "a0fe21c1-6950-3f02-b144-092ca13073cb",
                                "text": "Interspecific associations are currently believed to take part in evolution by improving a partner's fitness through integrated endosymbioses or by causing reproductive isolation and subsequent host speciation, such as in the Wolbachia endosymbioses (Nardon and Grenier, 1991; Margulis, 1993a; Bordenstein et al., 2001)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "49d08b3d-b5c6-40e9-a820-e72411d3a9c4",
                                "sense_id": "a0fe21c1-6950-3f02-b144-092ca13073cb",
                                "text": "2014, Robert E. Blankenship, Molecular Mechanisms of Photosynthesis, Wiley Blackwell, 2nd Edition, page 226,\nEvidence is now overwhelming that several groups of eukaryotic algae originated from a secondary endosymbiosis, in which a eukaryotic alga was incorporated into a second host (Palmer and Delwiche, 1996; Delwiche and Palmer, 1997; Delwiche, 1999; Keeling, 2010, 2013; Curtis et al., 2012)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "43fabf1a-534b-48ec-a801-0fa8461c9988",
                                "sense_id": "a0fe21c1-6950-3f02-b144-092ca13073cb",
                                "text": "The second chapter (by David Smith) describes the primary endosymbiosis as an introduction for secondary endosymbioses. He illustrates the origin of primary endosymbiosis, plastid genome evolution, and loss of photosynthesis in some lineages of Archaeplastida."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "aff68a10-0bcd-40f7-bb5e-1c20066f9506",
                        "source_entry_id": "f5c4c3d0-e4bf-3713-b70d-499901e256df",
                        "source_sense_id": "a0fe21c1-6950-3f02-b144-092ca13073cb",
                        "source_sense_description": "living within another organism",
                        "target_lang_word": "endosymbiose",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "endosymbiose",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "42b14705-cc4f-3991-a752-51be4bbdfb74",
                                        "entry_id": "270938c9-cef4-3400-b727-17f21adf2995",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Symbiose où l’un des deux organismes coopérants est contenu par l’autre."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a572df02-ef44-43c3-ac1a-ac66f5c616be",
                                                "sense_id": "42b14705-cc4f-3991-a752-51be4bbdfb74",
                                                "text": "L’un des arguments qui a fait pencher la balance en faveur de l’endosymbiose est l'absence de flagelle chez les Rickettsiales (la probable famille de bactéries de l'ancêtre des mitochondries), donc un manque de mobilité chez les aïeuls de l'organite cellulaire."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7bf87519-0bac-46ae-a5d9-a1a465942299",
                                                "sense_id": "42b14705-cc4f-3991-a752-51be4bbdfb74",
                                                "text": "Cependant, John McCutcheon, de l’Université du Montana, à Missoula aux États-Unis, a découvert un cas étonnant où l’endosymbiose concerne trois espèces imbriquées, à la façon de poupées russes !"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
